.TH PTY_SCREEN_DUMP 3 2026-10-03 "libptytest 0.1" "libptytest Manual"
.SH NAME
pty_screen_dump, pty_screen_dump_ansi, pty_region_dump_c \- print the
screen for a person, a terminal, or a test
.SH SYNOPSIS
.nf
.B #include <ptytest.h>
.PP
.BI "void pty_screen_dump(PtySession *" s ", FILE *" fp );
.BI "void pty_screen_dump_ansi(PtySession *" s ", FILE *" fp );
.BI "void pty_region_dump_c(PtySession *" s ", const PtyRegion *" reg ", FILE *" fp );
.fi
.SH DESCRIPTION
.BR pty_screen_dump ()
prints the screen as text, untrimmed and framed, with row numbers, a
column ruler and the cursor position and visibility.
The assertion macros print it when a text search fails.
.PP
.BR pty_screen_dump_ansi ()
prints the screen with its attributes and colours as escape sequences:
.B cat
on the file in a terminal shows the screen as the program showed it,
which a plain dump cannot do for a selection bar or an error in red.
Rows are separated by a reset and CR LF and the last row has no line
end, so feeding the dump to a session of the same size with
.BR pty_feed (3)
reproduces the screen exactly \(em a saved failure can be loaded back
and inspected with every function of the library.
.PP
.BR pty_region_dump_c ()
prints a region
.RI ( reg
NULL: the whole screen) as a C array literal that can be pasted into a
test as its expectation.
Trailing blanks of each row and trailing blank rows are left out; quotes
and backslashes are escaped.
Take a snapshot only of a screen that has been checked by eye: a snapshot
of a wrong screen makes a test that passes on the bug.
.SH EXAMPLE
.EX
/* Keep the evidence of a failed run next to the build. */
FILE *fp = fopen("build/_test/failed\-inbox.ansi", "w");
if (fp) {
    pty_screen_dump_ansi(s, fp);
    fclose(fp);
}
.EE
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR pty_region_compare (3),
.BR pty_feed (3),
.BR pty_assert (3)
